<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercisegroup xml:id="exercise-computing-definite-integrals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Computing definite integrals
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Use the <xref ref="theorem-second-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus" text="title"/> to compute the following Riemann integrals.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the <xref ref="theorem-second-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus" text="title"/> to compute the Riemann integral
        
        <md>
          \int_{ 0 }^{ 3 }{ 2 x + 1 \, \diff{ x } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m> \int_{ 0 }^{ 3 }{ 2 x + 1 \, \diff{ x } } = 12 </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Consider the real-valued function <m> f \colon \clint{ 0 }{ 3 } \to \R </m> defined by the formula <m> f \of{ x } = x^{ 2 } + x </m>. Since <m> f </m> is continuously differentiable with derivative <m> f' \of{ x } = 2 x + 1 </m>, we see that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_{ 0 }^{ 3 }{ 2 x + 1 \, \diff{ x } } \amp = \int_{ 0 }^{ 3 }{ f' \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = f \of{ 3 } - f \of{ 0 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = 12 - 0 = 12
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        by the <xref ref="theorem-second-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the <xref ref="theorem-second-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus" text="title"/> to compute the Riemann integral
        
        <md>
          \int_{ -1 }^{ 2 }{ y^{ 3 } + 3 y \, \diff{ y } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m> \int_{ -1 }^{ 2 }{ y^{ 3 } + 3 y \, \diff{ y } } = \frac{ 33 }{ 4 } </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Consider the real-valued function <m> g \colon \clint{ -1 }{ 2 } \to \R </m> defined by the formula <m> g \of{ y } = \frac{ y^{ 4 } }{ 4 } + \frac{ 3 y^{ 2 } }{ 2 } </m>. Since <m> g </m> is continuously differentiable with derivative <m> g' \of{ y } = y^{ 3 } + 3 y </m>, we see that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_{ -1 }^{ 2 }{ y^{ 3 } + 3 y \, \diff{ y } } \amp = \int_{ -1 }^{ 2 }{ g' \of{ y } \, \diff{ y } } = g \of{ 2 } - g \of{ -1 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = 7 - \of{ -\frac{ 5 }{ 4 } } = \frac{ 33 }{ 4 }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        by the <xref ref="theorem-second-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the <xref ref="theorem-second-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus" text="title"/> to compute the Riemann integral
        
        <md>
          \int_{ 1 }^{ 3 }{ 2^{ z } \, \diff{ z } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m> \int_{ 1 }^{ 3 }{ 2^{ z } \, \diff{ z } } = \frac{ 7 }{ \ln \of{ 2 } } </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Consider the real-valued function <m> h \colon \clint{ 1 }{ 3 } \to \R </m> defined by the formula <m> h \of{ z } = \frac{ 2^{ z } }{ \ln \of{ 2 } } </m>. Since <m> h </m> is continuously differentiable with derivative <m> h' \of{ z } = 2^{ z } </m>, we see that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_{ 1 }^{ 3 }{ 2^{ z } \, \diff{ z } } \amp = \int_{ 1 }^{ 3 }{ h' \of{ z } \, \diff{ z } } = h \of{ 3 } - h \of{ 1 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \frac{ 2^{ 3 } }{ \ln \of{ 2 } } - \frac{ 2^{ 1 } }{ \ln \of{ 2 } } = \frac{ 7 }{ \ln \of{ 2 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        by the <xref ref="theorem-second-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercisegroup>
